(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["principles-of-diagnostic-medicine-i-exam-2-cardiac-imaging-quiz"]={"title":"Cardiac Imaging Quiz 1 — Principles of Diagnostic Medicine I","category":"Principles of Diagnostic Medicine I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What is done before a cardiovascular magnetic resonance study is ordered?","choices":["A coronary angiogram","A nuclear perfusion study","An echocardiogram","A coronary calcium score"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It is the first-line functional study, and magnetic resonance answers what it leaves unresolved rather than replacing it. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"What can be done during a coronary angiogram that cannot be done during any of the noninvasive studies?","choices":["Treatment, by percutaneous coronary intervention there and then","Measurement of ejection fraction","Assessment of myocardial viability","Calcium scoring"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is the one study that can fix what it finds while the catheter is still in place. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Which patient factors, other than oesophageal disease, contraindicate a transoesophageal study?","choices":["An uncooperative patient, restricted neck movement, airway risk","Significant renal impairment","An implanted cardiac pacemaker","Atrial fibrillation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Two are about whether the patient can be positioned and kept still, and one is about whether the airway can be protected under sedation. In full: altered mental status or an uncooperative patient, cervical spine arthritis with reduced range of motion, and obstructive sleep apnoea or other risk of airway compromise. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Tracer uptake in a region is normal at rest but reduced after stress. What does that pattern indicate?","choices":["Infarcted, non-viable tissue","Inducible ischaemia, from a coronary stenosis","Normal myocardium","A pericardial effusion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The tissue is alive but cannot increase its supply when demand rises, which is what a stenosis does. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What does RIGHT heart catheterisation assess?","choices":["The coronary artery anatomy","Right-sided pressures, pulmonary resistance, valves and shunts","Aortic and mitral valve function","Systemic vascular resistance"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The pulmonary side of the circulation, and the shunts that connect the two sides. In full: right atrial and right ventricular pressure, pulmonary artery and occlusion pressure, pulmonary vascular resistance, tricuspid and pulmonic valve function, and intracardiac shunts. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"How does an exercise stress test detect myocardial ischaemia?","choices":["Directly, by imaging myocardial perfusion during exercise","By measuring coronary artery calcium before and after exercise","Indirectly, by continuous electrocardiographic monitoring","By measuring ejection fraction at peak exercise"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It never images the heart at all; it infers ischaemia from what the electrocardiogram does as demand rises, which is why the word is indirectly. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A region of myocardium shows no tracer uptake on either the stress or the rest images. What does that indicate?","choices":["Dead tissue","Inducible ischaemia","Normal myocardium","An artefact of the gamma camera"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Uptake requires functional myocardium, so tissue that never lights up is not ischaemic but infarcted. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What is the standing of chest radiography in cardiac evaluation?","choices":["It is not the primary modality, but it gives useful clues when obtained","It is the primary modality for assessing cardiac function","It has no role in cardiac assessment","It replaces echocardiography when the patient cannot lie flat"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is rarely the study you order to answer a cardiac question, and frequently the study you already have, which is why reading it well matters. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which exercise modalities are used, and which is preferred among the cycles?","choices":["Treadmill only","Arm ergometry, as first choice","Step testing","Treadmill and cycle ergometer"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and arm ergometry exists for patients who cannot use their legs, which keeps the test available to more people. With the stationary bicycle preferred. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which complications of exercise stress testing are described?","choices":["Contrast-induced nephropathy","Arrhythmia, angina, infarction and death","Malignancy induced by radiation exposure","Oesophageal perforation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The test deliberately provokes the thing it is looking for, which is why it is supervised and why it has a stopping rule. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"On what principle does radionuclide myocardial perfusion imaging work?","choices":["Diseased myocardium takes up more tracer than normal myocardium","Tracer is taken up only by scarred tissue","The tracer is metabolised into a contrast agent within the coronary arteries","Under stress, diseased myocardium receives less blood flow than normal myocardium"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, so comparing stress images against rest images is what makes the difference visible rather than either set alone. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"What does cardiovascular magnetic resonance avoid that computed tomography does not?","choices":["Dependence on the operator","Any need for a resting heart rate","Both ionising radiation and iodinated contrast","Sedation and airway monitoring"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Both avoided at once, which matters most in the young patient and in the patient with impaired kidneys. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A patient stops an exercise stress test having reached 70 per cent of their predicted maximum heart rate, with no electrocardiographic changes. How should the result be read?","choices":["A negative test, excluding ischaemia","A positive test","Inadequate to exclude ischaemia","Equivocal, requiring repeat in six months"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The threshold for a valid test is 85 per cent, so a negative result below it says nothing about the heart, only about the test. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which types of stress test are described?","choices":["Exercise and pharmacological only","Nuclear and magnetic resonance only","Treadmill and bicycle only","Standard exercise"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Two axes run through the list: how the heart is stressed, and how it is then imaged, and the four names are combinations of those two. Echocardiographic, nuclear and pharmacological. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What is obtained before an exercise stress test begins?","choices":["A resting echocardiogram in every case","A coronary calcium score","A resting electrocardiogram","A radionuclide injection at rest"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Without the resting baseline there is nothing to compare the exercise tracing against. Blood pressure both supine and standing. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which structures can be at least partly seen on a chest radiograph for cardiac purposes?","choices":["The coronary arteries and cardiac valves","The cardiac conduction system","The pericardial fluid volume","The cardiac silhouette"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Note what is absent from the list: the chambers, the valves and the coronary arteries are not among them. Great vessels, pulmonary vasculature, lungs and pleura. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which patients receive a pharmacological rather than an exercise stress test?","choices":["Those with a normal resting electrocardiogram","Those with renal impairment","Those who cannot exercise","Those under forty years of age"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The heart still has to be stressed; if the legs cannot do it, a drug does it instead. Usually because of a physical limitation. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What advantages does cardiac computed tomography have over echocardiography?","choices":["Assessment of cardiac function as well as anatomy","Superior resolution and noninvasive coronary assessment","Freedom from ionising radiation","Independence from heart rate and rhythm"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with fast acquisition and interpretation as well, which together make it the anatomical study of choice. It also shows internal structures directly, and acquires and reports quickly. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"On a vascular ultrasound, how does tissue density relate to the image?","choices":["The denser the structure the darker it appears","Density does not affect the image, only depth does","The denser the structure the lighter it appears","Only flowing blood produces any signal"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and superficial structures sit nearer the top of the screen, which together let you orient any image without being told what it is. So air is black and bone is near white. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What does electrophysiological testing do?","choices":["Assesses myocardial perfusion under stress conditions","Measures the cardiothoracic ratio","Visualises coronary anatomy","Investigates rhythm disorders and locates an arrhythmia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and where applicable it treats as well as diagnoses, by ablating the tissue it has just located. It reveals the precise location, and where applicable treats it by ablation in the same sitting. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Which is a listed indication for coronary angiography?","choices":["Screening an asymptomatic patient","Severe angina despite medical therapy","Suspected deep venous thrombosis","Newly diagnosed atrial fibrillation alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, together with acute infarction, a high-risk noninvasive test result, high-risk patients undergoing non-cardiac surgery, and arrhythmias suspected to be vascular in origin. Or unstable angina. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"What does LEFT heart catheterisation assess?","choices":["Coronary anatomy, aortic pressure and left ventricular function","Pulmonary artery pressure and vascular resistance","Tricuspid and pulmonic valve function","Right atrial pressure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Everything on the systemic side of the circulation, which is the side the left heart serves. Together with systemic vascular resistance, and aortic and mitral valve function, and left ventricular pressure. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Why is a transoesophageal study used in a patient presenting with cerebral ischaemia?","choices":["It measures cerebral blood flow directly and noninvasively","It can find thrombus in the left atrial appendage","It assesses the carotid arteries","It excludes coronary stenosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It looks for the source rather than the consequence, and the appendage is the site the transthoracic study sees worst. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which complications of coronary angiography occur at the catheter insertion site?","choices":["Contrast nephropathy and arrhythmia","Oesophageal perforation","Pneumothorax","Haematoma and pseudoaneurysm"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the pseudoaneurysm is the one to check for deliberately, since it maintains a continuous communication with the artery. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"What is the fundamental limitation of cardiac computed tomography compared with echocardiography and magnetic resonance?","choices":["It assesses anatomy but not function","It cannot show the coronary arteries","It cannot be used in patients with a normal heart rate","It requires an endoscope"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It shows the structure in great detail and cannot tell you how well it is working, which decides when it is the wrong study. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Why does the cardiothoracic ratio require a properly performed posteroanterior film?","choices":["Projection alters the apparent size of the cardiac silhouette","Only a posteroanterior film shows the pulmonary vasculature","Contrast is required and is only given for posteroanterior films","The ratio can only be calculated with electrocardiographic gating"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A ratio measured off the wrong projection is a measurement of the technique rather than of the heart. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What does catheter ablation do during electrophysiological testing?","choices":["Stimulates the area to restore normal conduction","Inactivates part of the heart by destroying that tissue","Places a permanent pacing wire in the atrium","Delivers a drug directly to the arrhythmic focus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The tissue is not merely silenced but destroyed, which is what makes the treatment definitive. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"What is cephalisation on a chest radiograph?","choices":["Fluid tracking into the interlobar fissures","The trachea deviating toward the apex","Calcification of the aortic arch","Upper zone vessels becoming larger"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Flow redistributes upward, which is why the sign sits in the lungs while the problem is in the heart. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"When is an exercise stress test terminated?","choices":["At maximum performance","Only when the patient asks to stop","After a fixed twelve minutes regardless of response","When blood pressure returns to baseline"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Three separate stopping conditions, only one of which is the patient simply having had enough. On signs or symptoms of ischaemia, or at a predetermined endpoint. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"How is coronary angiography performed?","choices":["Under general anaesthesia with the patient intubated","Under local anaesthesia with intravenous sedation","Without contrast, using ultrasound guidance","Through the oesophagus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and pressures are measured as the catheter goes, so it yields haemodynamic information alongside the pictures. With catheters guided by fluoroscopy and contrast injected into each coronary artery. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 72"}]};
